{{also|Teeter}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Alteration of {{m|en|titter#Etymology_1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtiːtə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-teeter.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtitəɹ/|[ˈtʰiɾɚ]|a=GA}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To tilt back and forth on an edge.
#: {{ux|en|He '''teetered''' on the brink of the precipice.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en
|title=How our home delivery habit reshaped the world|author=Samanth Subramanian|date=November 21 2019|journal=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/nov/21/how-our-home-delivery-habit-reshaped-the-world
|passage=The concrete floors of B2B sheds were already being built to an exacting degree of flatness, calibrated using lasers, so that forklifts would not '''teeter''' while lifting pallets to the highest shelves.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-05-04|author=w:Frank Bruni|title=Republicans Are Running Wild in My State|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/05/04/opinion/north-carolina-republicans.html|passage=This is not a land of blowouts. It’s a middle ground, and that’s reflected in voter registration rolls. Nearly 2.6 million North Carolinians declare themselves unaffiliated, while just over 2.4 million identify as Democrats and just under 2.2 million as Republicans. We don’t tilt. We '''teeter'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[totter]] {{gloss|move unsteadily}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=28 Dec 85|volume=13|number=24|journal=Gay Community News|page=12|author=Nora Janeway|title=Women's Pentagon Action Meets for the Last Time|text=How many other organizations have sprung up amidst apparently unlimited energy, been the focus of a great deal of activism and education, and then begun to diminish? Some '''teeter''' along for a long time even though their base of support seems to have eroded; some metamorphose into something else entirely. Others die, and their death may be more or less peaceful, more or less guilt-riddled.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To be [[indecisive]]; to [[hesitate]].
#: {{ux|en|We '''teetered''' on the fence about buying getaway tickets and missed the opportunity.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To be close to becoming a typically negative situation.
#: {{ux|en|Despite appearances, the firm was '''teetering''' on the edge of bankruptcy.}}

